    thinking: "**Updating Photo Arrays**\n\nI\'m currently focused on populating the `galleryPhotos` array within `src/data/weddingData.ts`. My aim is to ensure each tab, namely \"Couple,\" \"Bride,\" and \"Groom,\" has a sufficient number of images \342\200\223 specifically, five photos per tab. I\'m prioritizing the \"Couple\" tab, followed by the \"Bride\" and \"Groom\" tabs.\n\n\n**Analyzing Background Visibility**\n\nI\'m now assessing background visibility across different website sections, particularly addressing the user\'s comment about seeing only a white background. I\'m focusing on the Hero and Story sections. The plan involves incorporating high-quality background images and refining overlay techniques. Specifically, I intend to replace the current opacity settings with a high-visibility background image combined with a subtle dark or warm cinematic overlay to enhance readability.\n\n\n**Enhancing Visual Clarity**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on improving visual clarity in the \"Thi\341\273\207p M\341\273\235i\" (Invitation) and \"Gallery\" sections. For the invitation, I\'m setting a backdrop that makes the background image of the elegant palace fully visible. It\'s a `bg-stone-950/70` overlay that will give it a vivid, elegant feel. The invitation card will sit on top, like a real invitation. Similarly, for the gallery, I\'m working with a background of wedding flowers, coupled with a dark/warm gradient scrim (`bg-stone-950/70`) to create a romantic ambiance.\n\n\n**Visualizing Section Overlays**\n\nI\'m now implementing the new section backgrounds. I\'m starting with the RSVP form, using a sunset wedding vows image with a dark overlay, ensuring the photo\'s presence and making the white RSVP form pop. I\'m continuing this approach with the Guestbook and GiftBox sections, utilizing appropriate images and overlays to create a cohesive and atmospheric design.\n\n\n"
